---
import LessonLayout from "../layouts/LessonLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import QuestionAnswer from "../components/QuestionAnswer.astro";
---

<LessonLayout
  title="Camera distance and perspective"
  description="Camera distance changes how large nearby objects look compared with the background. Learn when to move closer, when to step back and how to compare your results."
>
  <h2>Decide what should stand out</h2>
  <p>
    A foreground rock can anchor a landscape or take attention away from it.
    Before changing lenses, look at its size compared with the mountain, trees
    or waterfall behind it. If the balance feels wrong, try changing your
    distance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Move closer to make the rock more dominant. Step back to reduce its size
    compared with the background. This changes perspective: the way objects at
    different distances relate in your photograph.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a few steps can make a difference</h2>
  <p>
    Imagine a rock two metres in front of your camera and a ridge roughly 200
    metres away. Step back two metres. You've doubled your distance to the rock,
    but barely changed your distance to the ridge.
  </p>
  <p>
    With the same focal length and camera direction, the rock will appear about
    half as tall. The ridge will appear only slightly smaller. The ridge now has
    more presence compared with the rock, even though neither object moved.
  </p>
  <p>
    That's why small movements matter near a subject. Farther away, the same
    two-metre step produces a smaller change. You may need to walk farther to
    see a clear difference between two distant layers.
  </p>

  <h2>Move closer for a stronger foreground</h2>
  <p>
    A close viewpoint works when nearby details deserve attention. Rocks, roots
    and patches of flowers can give the viewer a place to start before looking
    towards the background. Choose a detail that supports the scene rather than
    filling the foreground simply because it's empty.
  </p>
  <p>
    Move forward a little, then check the edges of the frame. Is the rock hiding
    the shoreline? Are flowers covering the base of a waterfall? A larger
    foreground isn't useful if it blocks the feature you wanted to photograph.
  </p>
  <p>
    You may need a shorter focal length to include enough background from this
    close position. The lens provides the wider view; your position creates the
    stronger near-to-far size difference.
  </p>

  <h2>Step back to give the background more presence</h2>
  <p>
    If a mountain looks small behind a large rock, move farther from the rock.
    Watch their size relationship as you walk. The foreground will become less
    dominant, allowing the mountain to carry more of the photograph.
  </p>
  <p>
    Stepping back also makes the overall scene smaller in the frame. Once you
    like the perspective, try a longer focal length to tighten the composition.
    Check the mountain summit and frame edges so you don't crop out useful
    details.
  </p>
  <p>
    This can make the layers seem closer together. You'll often hear this called
    compression. Moving back changes the perspective; the longer lens helps
    restore the framing. It doesn't physically bring the background closer.
  </p>

  <Callout title="Move for perspective. Zoom for framing.">
    <p>
      First choose the relationship between foreground and background. Then
      adjust focal length to decide how much of the scene to include.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Compare similar framing</h2>
  <p>
    It's easier to judge perspective when the foreground object stays about the
    same size in both images. Take one photograph close to the rock with a wider
    view. Then step back and use a longer focal length until the rock looks
    similarly sized in the frame.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now compare the background. In the second photograph, it should look larger
    relative to the rock. Keep the camera height and direction as consistent as
    practical so you aren't also comparing a large change in angle.
  </p>

  <h2>Check overlap, height and focus</h2>
  <p>
    Distance won't solve every composition problem. If two trees overlap, a
    sideways step may separate them more effectively than walking backwards. If
    a rock hides a path, raising the camera may reveal it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Focus is a separate check. A close foreground and distant background may not
    both look sharp with your chosen aperture and focus distance. Review the
    photograph at a larger view rather than assuming that a good size balance
    also means everything is in focus.
  </p>
  <p>
    Work from stable ground. Don't step towards a cliff edge, into moving water
    or onto fragile plants to get a stronger foreground. Change your subject or
    viewpoint when moving closer isn't practical.
  </p>

  <h2>Try it in the simulator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Reset the simulator and save the starting view as comparison A.</li>
    <li>
      Leave focal length at 35 mm. Reduce camera distance from 10 m to 6 m.
      Notice how the rock grows relative to the mountain.
    </li>
    <li>
      Increase distance to 20 m. The rock becomes smaller relative to the
      mountain. Try 50 mm to tighten the framing.
    </li>
    <li>
      Save comparison B. Compare the height ratio and visible background, rather
      than judging only how much of the frame the rock fills.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    These settings show the effect in this illustrated scene. They're not lens
    or distance recommendations for every landscape. Outdoors, the spacing
    between objects determines how much movement you'll need.
  </p>

  <QuestionAnswer question="Can I change perspective by cropping instead of moving?">
    <p>
      No. Cropping removes the edges and enlarges what's left. It doesn't change
      the size relationship between objects in the original photograph. To
      change that relationship, take another photograph from a different
      position.
    </p>
  </QuestionAnswer>
  <QuestionAnswer question="How do I choose the right distance?">
    <p>
      Start with what you want the viewer to notice. Try a closer and a farther
      viewpoint, then compare the foreground size, background presence and
      overlap. Keep the version that shows your subject clearly; there's no
      single distance that works for every scene.
    </p>
  </QuestionAnswer>
  <p>
    <a href="/perspective-simulator/">Try the perspective simulator</a>
    {" or "}
    <a href="/perspective-challenges/">
      practise with the perspective challenges
    </a>
    .
  </p>
</LessonLayout>
